#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124180 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= f_{x}(x, y)i + f_{y}(x, y)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 0 + 1)i + (0 + 4y + 0)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)i + 4yj \\\\\n\\nabla f(1, 1) &= (2(1) + 1)i + 4(1)j \\\\\n&= 3i + 4j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , 1 )}{\\Vert  \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , 1 ) \\Vert} & = \\frac{3 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2} + {4}^{2}}} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{5} ( 3 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} )\\end{array}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124181 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$. Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=x^2/9+y^2/16-1$. A normal vector is $\\nabla F=\\langle2x/9,y/8\\rangle$, which at $(0,4)$ equals $\\langle0,1/2\\rangle$. The normal line is therefore vertical: $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle0,4\\rangle+t\\langle0,1/2\\rangle$. Its Cartesian equation is $x=0$. Parametric form avoids division by the zero horizontal component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124182 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing\n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})k \\\\\n&= (4x - y)i - xj - 2zk \\\\\n\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt{2}) &= (4 \\cdot 2 - 3)i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k \\\\\n&= 5i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) \\\\\n0 &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z\n$$\n\nour surface can be viewed as the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}(x, y, z)i + f_{y}(x, y, z)j + f_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)k \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2x - y}{x^{2} - xy})i + (\\frac{1}{y - x})j - k \\\\\n\\nabla f(2, 1, \\ln 2) &= (\\frac{2 \\cdot 2 - 1}{2^{2} - 2 \\cdot 1})i + (\\frac{1}{1 - 2})j - k \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}i - j - k \\\\\n&= \\langle \\frac{3}{2},-1,-1\\rangle \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{x - x_{0}}{v_{x}} = \\frac{y - y_{0}}{v_{y}} = \\frac{z - z_{0}}{v_{z}}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{r}\\frac{x - 2}{( \\frac{3}{2} )} = \\frac{y - 1}{- 1} = \\frac{z - \\ln   2}{- 1} \\\\ \\frac{x - 2}{3} = \\frac{y - 1}{- 2} = \\frac{z - \\ln   2}{- 2} .\\end{array}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901739f6-6476-5b09-9d46-197bf5aaa672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y + 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122345} {:answer/id #uuid "f3107bf3-2907-5fee-b9d6-76c649c336b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122346} {:answer/id #uuid "218985c6-986f-5927-ac74-e7f1365b3d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x + y + 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122347} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4420b7-2d11-5d0b-af4b-bf52b0dda88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122348} {:answer/id #uuid "54c6ec15-8b9c-5bdd-8864-5a12ebc458b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b4420b7-2d11-5d0b-af4b-bf52b0dda88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122348} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b7b194-3101-5717-a973-1aa6c440f7cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aec682f0-94b9-5c3a-a680-f47e16fecc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36339" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy = 5x^{2} + y^{2}$. Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1, 1)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Write the curve as $F(x,y)=5x^2+y^2-6xy=0$. Then $\\nabla F=\\langle10x-6y,2y-6x\\rangle$, and $\\nabla F(1,1)=\\langle4,-4\\rangle$. The normal line passes through $(1,1)$ in this direction: $y-1=-(x-1)$, or $x+y-2=0$."}]}
